Title: Textbook chapter about the case report: From dog bite to dialysis: complement-mediated haemolytic uraemic syndrome

## Thrombotic Microangiopathy in Infection: Concepts, Classifications, and Complement Biology

Thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) is a clinicopathologic syndrome defined by the triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA), thrombocytopenia and end‑organ injury—most commonly the kidneys and brain. The hemolysis is mechanical, reflected by schistocytes on the peripheral smear, elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), low haptoglobin and often reticulocytosis. The thrombocytopenia reflects platelet consumption in microvascular thrombi. Organ dysfunction ranges from oligo‑anuric acute kidney injury (AKI) to neurologic deficits, and the pattern of organ involvement helps to narrow the underlying etiology.

TMAs are not a single disease but a group of overlapping entities that require different, often mutually exclusive, therapies. The major categories to consider in adults are:
- Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP): characteristically caused by severe deficiency of the von Willebrand factor‑cleaving protease ADAMTS13 (activity <10%). Clinically, TTP often has prominent neurologic findings and responds urgently to plasma exchange.
- Shiga toxin–associated HUS (STEC‑HUS): usually follows an enteric infection with Shiga toxin–producing E. coli; diagnosis is supported by stool testing for Shiga toxin and a compatible prodrome.
- Complement‑mediated HUS (CM‑HUS; historically “atypical HUS”): results from dysregulation of the alternative complement pathway, often in patients with genetic variants or acquired autoantibodies to complement regulators. Terminal complement blockade (eculizumab) is the disease‑specific therapy.
- Secondary TMAs: a heterogeneous group in which systemic processes (sepsis/DIC, malignant hypertension, transplantation, pregnancy/postpartum, certain drugs, autoimmune disease) produce endothelial injury and TMA features. Management focuses on treating the underlying trigger; in some cases complement blockade is considered.

Understanding complement biology is central to CM‑HUS. The alternative pathway is constitutively active at a low level by a “tick‑over” mechanism in which spontaneous C3 hydrolysis generates C3b that can bind surfaces and form the alternative pathway C3 convertase (C3bBb). Complement regulators—soluble (factors H and I) and membrane‑bound (e.g., membrane cofactor protein/CD46)—normally restrict activation on host endothelium. Genetic variants or autoantibodies that impair these regulators permit uncontrolled amplification of C3/C5 convertases, generation of the membrane attack complex and release of anaphylatoxins. On microvascular endothelium this produces endothelial activation and injury, exposure of subendothelial matrix, platelet aggregation and formation of fibrin‑rich occlusive microthrombi—clinically manifest as MAHA, thrombocytopenia and tissue ischemia, especially in the kidney. Importantly, penetrance of complement gene variants is incomplete; most patients require a secondary trigger (infection, pregnancy, surgery, certain drugs) to precipitate clinical disease.

Infections can play a dual role: they are common triggers for CM‑HUS yet may also mimic or produce other hematologic phenomena such as disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Capnocytophaga canimorsus, the organism in the present case, is a slow‑growing, encapsulated gram‑negative rod from the oral flora of dogs and cats. It can cause fulminant sepsis, DIC and multiorgan failure even in previously healthy hosts. From a complement standpoint C. canimorsus shows variable serum susceptibility; some capsulated strains resist complement‑mediated killing. Thus, infection with this organism may both drive complement activation (providing the “second hit”) and, through capsular properties, challenge host defense—creating the clinical paradox of infection‑triggered complement overactivation that can culminate in CM‑HUS.

Distinguishing DIC from TMA is a key practical challenge, particularly in sepsis when both processes can coexist. DIC is a systemic consumptive coagulopathy characterized by prolonged prothrombin time (PT/INR), activated partial thromboplastin time, low fibrinogen, marked elevation of D‑dimer and bleeding diathesis; microthrombi are usually fibrinaceous and widespread. TMA, by contrast, classically presents with prominent MAHA and thrombocytopenia with relatively preserved coagulation tests (unless superimposed DIC exists). In practice a patient with sepsis may present initially with DIC and bleeding, and then develop or unmask a TMA once the consumptive coagulopathy begins to resolve—an important pattern illustrated by this case, where coagulation indices normalized while hemolysis and renal injury persisted or worsened. Key distinguishing features favoring a superimposed TMA are persistent or new schistocytosis, rising LDH with undetectable haptoglobin, sustained thrombocytopenia despite correction of coagulation, worsening renal function out of proportion to hemodynamics, and kidney biopsy showing glomerular/arteriolar thrombi with ischemic glomeruli and fibrin deposition.

A pragmatic diagnostic framework in a patient with MAHA, thrombocytopenia and AKI is:
- Rapidly obtain ADAMTS13 activity (and use clinical scores such as PLASMIC to prioritize empiric plasma exchange if suspicion is high); ADAMTS13 <10% points to TTP and mandates immediate PEX.
- Test for Shiga toxin (stool PCR or culture) when enteric prodrome is present.
- Look for secondary causes (sepsis, drugs, autoimmune disease, pregnancy, malignant hypertension).
- Consider CM‑HUS when ADAMTS13 is not severely reduced, Shiga toxin is negative and a TMA persists or progressive AKI occurs; remember that routine complement tests (C3/C4) can be normal. Extended complement functional and genetic testing can support the diagnosis but are often delayed and can be confounded by prior plasma exchange or complement blockade.
- Use kidney biopsy selectively, especially when diagnosis is unclear or to assess the degree of chronicity; classic findings in CM‑HUS include thrombi in glomerular capillaries and arterioles, endothelial swelling, mesangiolysis and ischemic changes.

Therapeutic implications follow directly from diagnosis. TTP needs urgent plasma exchange and often immunosuppression. STEC‑HUS is managed supportively. CM‑HUS benefits from early complement blockade with eculizumab, which reduces hemolysis and improves renal outcomes; however, initiating eculizumab during active bacteremia raises concern because the terminal complement pathway is critical for defense against encapsulated organisms (classically Neisseria meningitidis, but also other capsulated bacteria). When complement inhibition is contemplated, clinicians should strive to document microbiologic control, administer appropriate vaccines (meningococcal and, when indicated, Haemophilus and pneumococcal), and provide antimicrobial prophylaxis until effective vaccination or clearance of infection. Plasma exchange may provide temporary benefit in CM‑HUS—by removing circulating complement fragments or autoantibodies—but does not address the underlying complement dysregulation and may complicate timing of diagnostic testing and vaccination.

In summary, TMAs encompass a spectrum of disorders with overlapping clinical features but distinct pathobiology and divergent treatments. Infection is a frequent precipitant of TMA and—depending on the organism and host factors—may produce DIC, secondary TMA or trigger complement‑mediated HUS. A high index of suspicion is required when hemolysis and kidney injury persist or worsen despite resolution of sepsis or coagulopathy. Rapid, directed testing (ADAMTS13, Shiga toxin assays, peripheral smear for schistocytes) combined with a careful assessment for secondary causes allows timely institution of disease‑specific therapy and improves the chances of renal and hematologic recovery.

## The Case: Dog-Bite Sepsis, DIC, and Early Organ Failure

Four days after a seemingly minor dog bite, this previously well woman presented with an overwhelmingly septic picture: high fever (>40 °C), profound malaise, refractory hypoglycaemia (nadir 0.6 mmol/L), mottled cool extremities and rapidly evolving multiorgan dysfunction. Her initial laboratory panel reflected the severity of the systemic insult — marked inflammatory response, pancytopenia, coagulopathy, metabolic acidosis (pH 7.27) with an elevated lactate (5.7 mmol/L), and rising creatinine with proteinuria and haematuria. This constellation pointed to fulminant sepsis complicated by disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and early acute kidney injury (AKI), and rightly prompted urgent, parallel priorities: identify the pathogen, control any surgical nidus, and stabilise organ function.

The first critical step was obtaining blood cultures and beginning broad empiric antimicrobial therapy directed at the typical pathogens of dog bites. Capnocytophaga canimorsus, Pasteurella species, staphylococci/streptococci and anaerobes are all relevant; piperacillin–tazobactam is a reasonable initial choice because it covers gram‑negatives and anaerobes while providing robust empirical coverage for severe bite-related infections. In this case, C. canimorsus was suspected from the outset and later confirmed, but diagnosis was delayed by the organism’s slow, fastidious growth — a practical reminder that negative early cultures do not exclude a clinically significant zoonotic pathogen and that molecular methods (PCR or sequencing) can be useful when cultures remain negative or growth is delayed.

Concurrently, clinicians must rapidly assess for a surgically remediable source. Dog-bite injuries can be complicated by deep soft‑tissue infection or necrotising fasciitis; prompt surgical evaluation and imaging were used here to exclude that possibility. With no surgical target identified, focus turned to medical resuscitation and support.

Management in the intensive care unit emphasised careful haemodynamic and organ support. Initial fluid resuscitation was required to treat distributive shock and maintain perfusion, but was titrated with awareness of the developing cardiorenal compromise: the patient developed septic cardiomyopathy with impaired cardiac function and progressive volume overload. Because she became anuric despite diuretics and fluids, continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) was started both to replace renal function and to provide precise volume control in the face of cardiac dysfunction. This sequence—aggressive resuscitation followed by early recognition of fluid intolerance and timely CRRT—illustrates the balance needed when fluids are life‑saving but can worsen pulmonary or cardiac failure.

The coagulopathy required focused supportive measures. Her presentation was consistent with DIC — consumptive thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen and clinical microthrombotic phenomena with ecchymoses and distal ischemic changes. Management of DIC centres on treating the underlying cause (here, sepsis) while replacing critical components when bleeding or invasive procedures demand it. Platelet transfusions and fibrinogen replacement were administered as clinically indicated to control bleeding and to allow invasive monitoring or procedures when necessary. Routine prophylactic transfusion is not indicated in DIC; thresholds should be guided by haemorrhage, planned interventions and laboratory parameters.

The profound pancytopenia prompted a parallel haematologic evaluation to exclude primary marrow processes such as acute leukaemia or haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH), both of which may present with fever, cytopenias and coagulopathy. Haematology input was obtained and initial flow cytometry did not reveal blasts or aberrant populations; a planned bone‑marrow biopsy was deferred when testing did not support a primary marrow disorder. Severe infections themselves — notably C. canimorsus — can cause leukopenia and marrow suppression, underscoring the need to consider infection‑mediated marrow dysfunction in the differential.

Once blood cultures eventually grew Capnocytophaga canimorsus, antibiotics were narrowed according to susceptibility: de‑escalation to ampicillin (and later oral phenoxymethylpenicillin in this patient’s course) was appropriate because most C. canimorsus isolates are penicillin‑sensitive. The clinical approach — early broad empiric therapy, timely cultures, surgical assessment, organ‑supportive care in the ICU and targeted transfusion support for DIC — together represent the standard priorities when a patient presents with dog‑bite sepsis complicated by coagulopathy and early organ failure. Awareness of C. canimorsus’ slow growth and the value of molecular diagnostics can shorten the time to definitive identification in future similar cases.

## Unmasking Superimposed TMA: Diagnostic Reasoning and Standards

The clinical turning point in this case was not the admission diagnosis of fulminant sepsis but a secondary, abrupt haematologic change: after apparent improvement from Capnocytophaga canimorsus bacteraemia and resolving coagulopathy, the patient developed a rapid, isolated fall in haemoglobin together with persistent thrombocytopenia, a rising lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and an undetectable haptoglobin. Those features are the laboratory signature of microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA), and they should immediately prompt reconsideration of superimposed thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA). Understanding how to interpret and sequence the available tests — and when to proceed to kidney biopsy — is essential because early recognition changes management and prognosis.

MAHA is a laboratory and morphological diagnosis rather than one based on a single serologic result. A falling haemoglobin with high LDH, indirect hyperbilirubinaemia, low or absent haptoglobin and an inadequate reticulocyte response together indicate ongoing intravascular haemolysis. The peripheral smear is pivotal: fragmented erythrocytes (schistocytes) provide direct evidence of mechanical red-cell destruction in the microcirculation. While reporting standards vary, even a small but reproducible increase in schistocytes is important; in this patient a marked 15–20% schistocytosis left little doubt that MAHA was present. Importantly, a positive direct antiglobulin test (DAT/Coombs) can mislead. Drugs such as penicillin and recent transfusions commonly produce a transient IgG‑positive DAT without true autoimmune haemolysis. In this case the initial IgG-positive DAT was likely drug- or transfusion-related and converted to negative on repeat testing. Clinicians must avoid anchoring to a single positive DAT when the clinical and morphological picture supports MAHA/TMA.

Once MAHA and thrombocytopenia are documented, rapid exclusion of treatable and time‑sensitive causes is the immediate priority. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is the critical diagnosis to rule out because it is medical emergency requiring urgent plasma exchange (PEX). Clinical tools such as the PLASMIC score provide useful pretest probability while awaiting ADAMTS13 activity; a low score reduces the likelihood of TTP but cannot definitively exclude it. Definitive laboratory diagnosis rests on ADAMTS13 activity: severe deficiency (<10%) is consistent with TTP, whereas values well above that threshold (for example, 43.5% as in this patient) make TTP unlikely. Because ADAMTS13 assays often take time to return, empirical PEX should be started when TTP cannot be confidently excluded based on clinical assessment and scoring, because delays in PEX in true TTP lead to poor outcomes. It is also important to recognize that PEX is a temporizing measure in complement-mediated HUS (CM-HUS) — it may transiently remove mediators or autoantibodies and can stabilise the patient, but it does not target the underlying complement dysregulation.

Shiga-toxin–associated HUS (STEC-HUS) is another consideration when MAHA and AKI coexist. Stool PCR for Shiga toxin and a compatible diarrhoeal prodrome help exclude this diagnosis. In our patient the absence of an enteric prodrome and negative stool PCR argued strongly against STEC-HUS.

Because CM-HUS is a diagnosis of exclusion, complement testing and genetic evaluation are important parts of the workup, but their interpretation requires nuance. Routine complement levels (C3 and C4) are often normal in CM-HUS and therefore cannot be used to exclude the condition. Functional assays of complement activity (for example, AH50) may detect pathway dysfunction but are susceptible to confounding by treatments: both PEX and eculizumab alter circulating complement components and functional assays, producing results that may reflect treatment effects rather than the pre‑morbid state. Accordingly, when feasible, obtain baseline blood samples for complement assays and for ADAMTS13 before initiating PEX or eculizumab; when this is not possible, interpret post‑treatment results cautiously. Genetic testing for complement regulatory gene variants or anti‑factor H autoantibodies is useful for prognostication and to guide long‑term therapy decisions — detection of pathogenic variants increases relapse risk and supports longer duration of complement blockade — but absence of a pathogenic variant does not exclude CM-HUS, nor is genetic confirmation required to make the clinical diagnosis and start therapy.

Kidney biopsy serves a selective but decisive role when diagnostic uncertainty persists, when renal failure is severe and when histology will affect management. In TMA the renal biopsy classically shows glomerular and arteriolar endothelial swelling, capillary wall widening with intra‑luminal fibrin/platelet thrombi, mesangiolysis, and variable ischaemic changes in glomeruli and tubules; immunofluorescence is typically negative for immune complex deposition and electron microscopy does not show electron‑dense immune deposits — findings that help distinguish TMA from immune‑complex glomerulonephritides. In this patient the biopsy demonstrated ischaemic glomeruli with fibrin thrombi and severe acute tubular necrosis without immune complexes, supporting the diagnosis of TMA. Biopsy timing must balance diagnostic benefit against bleeding risk: thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy should be corrected (or biopsy postponed) until safe thresholds are met, and the procedure should be undertaken only when results are likely to influence therapy.

A frequent and clinically important dilemma is distinguishing sepsis‑associated disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) from superimposed CM-HUS. Both share thrombocytopenia and microthrombi, but they diverge in several respects. DIC is a consumptive coagulopathy with prolonged prothrombin and activated partial thromboplastin times, low fibrinogen, markedly elevated D‑dimer levels and a clinical phenotype dominated by bleeding and diffuse microvascular thrombosis driven by systemic coagulation activation. In contrast, TMA is a microvascular endothelial injury syndrome with predominant MAHA (schistocytes and haemolysis) and thrombocytopenia while routine coagulation tests (PT/aPTT) are often near normal. The temporal pattern is also informative: in this patient, coagulation parameters and inflammatory markers improved as the infection was treated, but haemolysis and thrombocytopenia persisted or recurred — a pattern that favours a secondary, complement‑driven TMA rather than ongoing DIC.

Translating these principles into action: when a patient with recent sepsis develops new anaemia and persistent thrombocytopenia, first confirm MAHA with haemolysis labs and a peripheral smear. Concurrently obtain coagulation studies, blood cultures if infection is active, ADAMTS13 and baseline complement samples before any PEX or complement inhibitor is given. If TTP is possible, initiate PEX without waiting for ADAMTS13 results. If ADAMTS13 excludes TTP and STEC testing is negative, consider CM-HUS and, where appropriate, proceed with targeted therapy such as eculizumab after weighing infectious risks and vaccinating or providing prophylactic antibiotics as indicated. Reserve kidney biopsy for cases where histologic confirmation will change management or where the renal lesion requires characterization, ensuring procedural safety in the thrombocytopenic patient.

In short, unmasking a superimposed TMA requires vigilance for the laboratory hallmarks of MAHA, a structured approach to exclude TTP and STEC‑HUS quickly, an understanding of the limitations of complement testing after therapeutic interventions, and selective use of kidney biopsy to confirm TMA and exclude alternative renal pathologies. Prompt, sequential application of these diagnostic standards underpinned the correct diagnosis and appropriate therapy in this patient, and they should guide clinicians faced with similar diagnostic complexity.

## Therapeutic Strategy for CM‑HUS in the Context of Recent Bacteremia

Managing complement‑mediated haemolytic uraemic syndrome (CM‑HUS) that arises or becomes evident shortly after bacteraemia forces clinicians to balance two competing imperatives: arresting a rapidly destructive complement‑driven thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) and avoiding suppression of a complement system that is still needed to control infection. The clinical course of the patient described—severe sepsis from Capnocytophaga canimorsus followed by delayed microangiopathic haemolysis, renal TMA on biopsy and response to complement blockade—illustrates practical principles that guide therapy in this setting.

When to use plasma exchange (PEX)
- Role and rationale. In adults presenting with MAHA, thrombocytopenia and AKI, the immediate priority is to exclude TTP because severe ADAMTS13 deficiency (<10%) requires urgent PEX. PEX is therefore appropriate as an immediate, potentially lifesaving intervention when TTP is suspected, while ADAMTS13 testing is pending. In CM‑HUS, PEX does not correct the underlying dysregulation of the alternative complement pathway, but it can remove circulating complement fragments, inflammatory mediators and (when present) anti‑factor H autoantibodies; thus it may produce transient hematologic and clinical improvement and is a reasonable temporizing measure.
- Practical use as a bridge. Use PEX as a bridge: start immediately when TTP cannot be excluded, in fulminant TMA, or when anti‑factor H antibodies are suspected. Recognize the benefit in CM‑HUS is usually temporary; definitive control generally requires complement blockade. In the case presented, PEX was used while TTP was ruled out and prior to eculizumab—diuresis returned transiently after PEX but sustained remission followed complement inhibition.

When and how to initiate complement blockade (eculizumab and alternatives)
- Eculizumab is the standard of care for CM‑HUS. Mechanism: monoclonal antibody that blocks terminal complement component C5, preventing formation of C5a and the membrane attack complex and thereby halting endothelial injury and microthrombus formation.
- Timing matters. Earlier initiation correlates with more rapid hematologic control and greater renal recovery. However, in the setting of active, culture‑proven bacteraemia with an encapsulated organism, immediate complement blockade can substantially increase risk of overwhelming infection. The decision is individualized:
  - If bacteraemia is controlled (negative cultures, clinical and biochemical improvement) but TMA persists or progresses, start eculizumab without delay.
  - If infection remains uncontrolled, treat aggressively with targeted antimicrobials and consider PEX as a temporizing measure; discuss risk/benefit with infectious diseases and nephrology. In life‑threatening TMA refractory to other measures, some teams will start eculizumab despite recent infection, with intensive antibiotic coverage and close monitoring.
- Dosing and alternatives. Typical induction (adult) regimens follow product labeling (in many centres: 900 mg IV weekly ×4, then 1200 mg IV every 2 weeks maintenance), as in this case. Ravulizumab is a longer‑acting C5 inhibitor with less frequent dosing and may be considered for maintenance once complement blockade is established and infection risk mitigated.

Vaccination, antibiotic prophylaxis and infection precautions
- Vaccination. Because terminal complement blockade substantially increases susceptibility to Neisseria meningitidis and may increase risk from other encapsulated organisms, administer meningococcal vaccines before starting eculizumab whenever possible:
  - MenACWY (conjugate A, C, W, Y) and MenB (serogroup B) are recommended, ideally ≥2 weeks prior to the first dose to allow protective immunity to develop.
  - If other encapsulated organisms are a concern or if immunization history is incomplete, consider pneumococcal and Hib vaccination as guided by infectious diseases or local recommendations.
- Urgent therapy and bridging antibiotics. If urgent eculizumab is indicated and vaccines cannot be given ≥2 weeks beforehand, begin appropriate antibiotic prophylaxis and continue until 2 weeks after vaccination (or longer per local guidance). Oral penicillin (phenoxymethylpenicillin/penicillin V or amoxicillin) is commonly used when organisms are penicillin‑susceptible; use an alternative based on allergies and local susceptibility patterns and involve infectious diseases. In the presented case, eculizumab was started after blood cultures had become negative and the patient received antibiotic prophylaxis.
- Patient education and safety netting. Counsel patients and caregivers about the increased risk of fulminant meningococcal disease: fever or acute illness should prompt immediate medical review. Provide written action plans and consider supplying emergency antibiotics where locally recommended.

Renal replacement therapy (RRT) and kidney‑focused care
- Indications and modality. Treat according to standard AKI/RRT indications: refractory hyperkalaemia, severe acidaemia, volume overload with cardiorespiratory compromise, severe uraemia, and certain toxin removals. Continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) is often used in haemodynamically unstable patients; intermittent haemodialysis can be used as stability permits.
- Weaning and monitoring for recovery. Monitor urine output (a key early sign of renal recovery), serum creatinine trends, solute control and acid–base status. Typical practical criteria to consider dialysis weaning include sustained urine output (>0.5–1.0 mL/kg/h with improving creatinine and electrolyte control) and clinical stability; decisions are individualized and coordinated with nephrology. In this patient, spontaneous diuresis returned after PEX sessions and further sustained recovery followed eculizumab.
- Preserve renal reserve. Avoid nephrotoxins (aminoglycosides, NSAIDs, contrast when possible), optimize haemodynamics, control blood pressure and treat sepsis aggressively to limit further renal injury.

Treatment duration and follow‑up
- Individualize duration of complement blockade. The optimal duration of eculizumab is individualized. Key determinants are the presence of a pathogenic complement gene variant or anti‑factor H antibodies, clinical severity, extent of renal recovery and patient preference. General considerations:
  - If pathogenic complement variants or anti‑factor H antibodies are present, many experts favour prolonged or lifelong therapy with close monitoring because relapse risk is high.
  - If no genetic predisposition is identified and the episode appears to be clearly triggered and singular (infection‑triggered CM‑HUS), a finite course (several months) with careful trial discontinuation after sustained remission may be reasonable.
- Structured monitoring. After stabilization, perform frequent laboratory monitoring to detect relapse early: CBC with platelets, LDH, haptoglobin (if available), creatinine, urinalysis and blood pressure—initially weekly or more frequently during induction or PEX changes, then spacing to monthly once stable. Reinstitute complement blockade promptly if hematologic or renal relapse occurs.
- Genetic and complement testing logistics. Obtain ADAMTS13, C3/C4 and genetic and functional complement testing early, ideally before PEX or eculizumab because both interventions confound results. If testing is delayed or performed after PEX/therapy, interpret values cautiously; coordinate with reference laboratories to time samples appropriately.

Adjunctive supportive care and complications management
- Haematologic support. Transfuse red cells for symptomatic anaemia. Avoid prophylactic platelet transfusion unless there is active bleeding or an urgent invasive procedure—platelet transfusions can worsen microthrombosis in TMA but are sometimes necessary; manage on a case‑by‑case basis with hematology input.
- Thrombosis and bleeding. TMA confers both thrombotic and bleeding risks, compounded by DIC in sepsis. Anticoagulation decisions require individualized risk–benefit assessment; prophylactic anticoagulation may be appropriate once platelets recover to a safe threshold (commonly >50 × 10^9/L for therapeutic anticoagulation, but institutional practice varies). In the presented case, bleeding manifestations and DIC were managed supportively as the coagulopathy resolved.
- Blood pressure and proteinuria. Control hypertension aggressively—ACE inhibitors or ARBs are useful for proteinuria and kidney protection once renal function and haemodynamics allow.
- Nutrition, rehabilitation and psychosocial support. Address malnutrition, deconditioning and psychosocial needs during recovery; prolonged hospitalisation and dialysis dependence carry significant functional and emotional impact.

Operational issues and laboratory interactions
- Interference of therapies with testing. Both PEX and eculizumab interfere with complement functional assays and with measurements of complement components; PEX can lower autoantibody titers and remove eculizumab; eculizumab can render assays of terminal pathway activity uninterpretable. Draw baseline genetic and functional complement tests before initiating PEX or eculizumab whenever feasible.
- Timing of eculizumab relative to PEX. Because PEX can remove eculizumab, when both are used concurrently give eculizumab after PEX and anticipate possible need for additional or adjusted dosing; discuss logistics with pharmacy and transfusion services.

Summary applied to the case
This patient’s care illustrates prudent application of these principles. PEX was employed early as a bridge while excluding TTP; ADAMTS13 activity effectively excluded TTP and anti‑factor H antibodies were not identified. Eculizumab was commenced after blood cultures were sterilized and after meningococcal vaccination were begun, with antibiotic prophylaxis administered because the vaccine interval was shortened. Following complement blockade the patient achieved hematologic remission and partial renal recovery. Duration of maintenance therapy is being individualized according to genetic testing results and the degree of renal recovery, with a plan for close laboratory and clinical surveillance and a low threshold to reinitiate therapy if relapse is suspected.

In sum, managing CM‑HUS after recent bacteremia requires early recognition of TMA, rapid exclusion of TTP, judicious use of PEX as a bridge, timely initiation of complement blockade when infection risk is controlled or acceptable, strict infection prophylaxis and vaccination strategies, careful renal support, and individualized decisions about duration of therapy informed by genetic testing and clinical course. Collaboration between nephrology, hematology, infectious diseases and transfusion services is essential for safe and effective care.

## Clinical Pearls, Outcomes, and Integration Into Practice

This case illustrates several practical and conceptual lessons about infection‑triggered complement‑mediated haemolytic uraemic syndrome (CM‑HUS) and highlights how recognition, testing strategy and timely therapy affect outcomes.

Recognize the pattern: In adults with severe sepsis, DIC and multiorgan dysfunction, a secondary TMA can be obscured. A high‑yield clinical clue is the emergence (or persistence) of microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA) with schistocytes, rising lactate dehydrogenase, undetectable haptoglobin and persistent thrombocytopenia accompanied by worsening or new acute kidney injury—especially when these signs appear or worsen after the initial septic picture begins to stabilise. In other words, haemolysis that develops late in the course, or does not correct with resolution of infection/DIC, should prompt an active search for TMA.

Capnocytophaga canimorsus: rare but important trigger and treatment implications: C. canimorsus is an uncommon but well‑documented trigger of fulminant sepsis and, rarely, of complement‑mediated TMA in previously healthy adults. Clinically, bites from dogs (or cats) with rapidly progressive systemic illness should raise suspicion for Capnocytophaga; empiric therapy for severe dog‑bite infections commonly includes agents active against Capnocytophaga (for example, piperacillin–tazobactam in the critically ill). For outpatient bite prophylaxis the recommended first‑line agent is amoxicillin–clavulanate. Early recognition of the infectious agent is important because active infection and bacteremia influence timing of complement blockade.

Don’t rely on C3/C4 alone to diagnose CM‑HUS: Standard serum complement levels (C3 and C4) are frequently normal in CM‑HUS and do not exclude disease. Likewise, functional pathway assays can be altered by plasma exchange or complement blockade and must be interpreted in context of recent treatments. Diagnosis is therefore clinical and exclusionary: demonstrate MAHA with schistocytes, exclude TTP (severely reduced ADAMTS13 activity <10%), exclude Shiga‑toxin HUS by stool testing, consider kidney biopsy when the diagnosis remains uncertain, and assess clinical response to targeted therapy.

Prioritise rapid differentiation from TTP and STEC‑HUS: TTP is time‑sensitive and requires immediate consideration because early plasma exchange (PEX) is lifesaving. Use clinical scores (eg, PLASMIC) to triage, obtain an ADAMTS13 level urgently, and start PEX empirically if TTP cannot be reliably excluded. STEC‑HUS should be considered when there is a compatible diarrhoeal prodrome; stool PCR for Shiga toxin is a rapid, high‑yield test.

Time matters for renal outcomes: Observational data and clinical experience indicate that earlier initiation of eculizumab (anti‑C5) in CM‑HUS is associated with better haematologic control and greater likelihood of renal recovery. PEX can provide transient benefit but does not address terminal pathway activation; use PEX while awaiting TTP exclusion or when an immune cause is suspected, but do not delay complement blockade unnecessarily when CM‑HUS is likely and infection is controlled.

Manage the infection‑therapy dilemma deliberately: Complement blockade increases susceptibility to infections with encapsulated organisms (classically Neisseria meningitidis), and terminal complement is part of defence against some Gram‑negative organisms, including Capnocytophaga. Best practice is to ensure source control and negative blood cultures prior to starting eculizumab when possible, to vaccinate against meningococci at least 2 weeks before blockade if clinically feasible, and to provide immediate antibiotic prophylaxis if vaccination timing is suboptimal. In practice, vaccination is frequently administered immediately before drug initiation (as in this case) with short‑term antibiotic prophylaxis continued until protective immunity is expected.

Multidisciplinary coordination is essential: Management decisions require input from nephrology, haematology, infectious diseases, critical care and pathology. This collaboration is key for timing of PEX, biopsy, complement testing, eculizumab initiation, vaccination strategy and antimicrobial prophylaxis.

Beware common pitfalls
- False‑positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) tests can occur after penicillin exposure or recent transfusion and can distract from an underlying MAHA; repeat testing and assessment of the peripheral smear for schistocytes are helpful.
- Complement assays (functional and component) are influenced by PEX and eculizumab; interpret values relative to treatment timing and obtain baseline samples before PEX when feasible.
- Improvement in diuresis after PEX does not prove the underlying mechanism—PEX may transiently remove circulating mediators but not correct dysregulated complement activation.

A pragmatic adult TMA workflow
1. Stabilise the patient (airway, breathing, circulation), correct life‑threatening abnormalities, and treat active infection.
2. Confirm MAHA on peripheral smear (schistocytes) and document thrombocytopenia and hemolytic markers (LDH, haptoglobin, reticulocytes).
3. Send urgent ADAMTS13 activity and assay for Shiga toxin (stool PCR). Begin PEX immediately if TTP cannot be excluded clinically.
4. Evaluate secondary causes (drugs, autoimmune disease, pregnancy, severe infection). Obtain basic complement studies (C3/C4), but do not delay therapy while awaiting results.
5. If TTP and STEC‑HUS are excluded or unlikely, and clinical suspicion for CM‑HUS is high (MAHA + thrombocytopenia + AKI with TMA features), discuss early eculizumab initiation. If ongoing bacteremia, strive for microbiological control before blockade; if blockade is needed urgently, ensure vaccination and provide antibiotic prophylaxis.
6. Consider kidney biopsy when the diagnosis is unclear or to define prognosis—histologic TMA supports the diagnosis and helps exclude other glomerulopathies.
7. Arrange genetics and anti‑complement antibody testing to inform prognosis and duration of therapy; collect samples prior to PEX/eculizumab when possible.

Genetic testing, prognosis and duration of therapy: Genetic variants in complement regulatory genes or autoantibodies are present in a substantial minority of CM‑HUS patients and increase relapse risk. A negative genetic panel lowers but does not eliminate risk of recurrence, particularly when an infectious trigger is identified. Genetic and antibody results should be integrated with clinical course when deciding on duration of complement blockade. Many centres tailor maintenance therapy duration to disease severity, renal recovery and genetic risk—continuing treatment for months to years in higher‑risk patients, with plans for careful discontinuation trials under close surveillance in lower‑risk cases.

Monitoring and follow‑up: During acute therapy monitor haemoglobin, platelets, schistocyte count, LDH, haptoglobin, serum creatinine, urine output and blood pressure frequently. After clinical remission, follow renal function, blood pressure and urinalysis for recurrence. Educate patients about infection risk, vaccination needs and prompt evaluation for fever.

Outcome integration from this case: In this patient, eradication of bacteremia and staged use of PEX followed by timely eculizumab led to haematologic remission and partial renal recovery with return of urine output and improvement in creatinine, although dialysis dependence persisted early on. The case underscores that complement blockade can be safely and effectively used after infection control, that kidney recovery can be partial but meaningful, and that decisions about maintenance therapy require individualized consideration of genetics, clinical course and patient preferences.

Takeaway: Maintain a low threshold to suspect TMA when haemolysis, thrombocytopenia and worsening renal function emerge or persist during or after sepsis. Rapid exclusion of TTP and STEC‑HUS, early multidisciplinary coordination and timely consideration of complement blockade—balanced against infection control and appropriate prophylaxis—are the practical steps most likely to improve outcomes in infection‑triggered CM‑HUS.